WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.043
Most days,

00:00:02.043 --> 00:00:04.879
...a lot of you don't hear too much about us.

00:00:04.879 --> 00:00:09.259
- Some days, these days, holidays,

00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:12.721
you might hear about maybe a handful of us.

00:00:12.721 --> 00:00:14.097
The first of us.

00:00:14.097 --> 00:00:16.516
- [Announcer:] For the first time in WNBA history

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:18.184
someone has dunked!
- [Cheering]

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.186
- The only.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:22.147
To win that title.

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:24.024
Take that job.

00:00:24.024 --> 00:00:25.734
Run that fast.

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:27.277
That far.

00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:30.739
These are luminaries in their own right,

00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:33.908
indisputably deserving the spotlight.

00:00:33.908 --> 00:00:35.660
But let's tell the rest of the story too:

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.289
how over-fixation on firsts

00:00:40.081 --> 00:00:43.460
to the exclusion of everything else can erase history,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.920
blot out the experiences of so many

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.091
who were, who are held back.

00:00:50.091 --> 00:00:52.135
How hyping just the same few names

00:00:52.135 --> 00:00:55.305
can feed scripts that pit women against each other,

00:00:55.305 --> 00:00:58.266
as if there were only so much attention to go around.

00:00:58.266 --> 00:01:02.020
But girls, women and allies

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.856
always have come together to make collective impacts

00:01:04.856 --> 00:01:06.191
and to make history.

00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:08.526
Protesting police brutality

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:11.237
and systemic racism.

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:15.116
Advocating to end policies that punish protest.

00:01:16.284 --> 00:01:17.869
Sitting out whole seasons

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:19.954
to focus on social justice.

00:01:19.954 --> 00:01:22.540
- [Cheering and clapping]

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.375
- Getting out to vote

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:26.795
and winning political seats as well.

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:29.047
And when one win sparks more?

00:01:29.047 --> 00:01:33.051
That ignites a radiance uncontained by any spotlight.

00:01:33.051 --> 00:01:35.428
We are speaking out against discrimination,

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.806
violence, objectification.

00:01:37.806 --> 00:01:40.475
We are fighting for representation,

00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:44.229
equal pay and parental leave protection.

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:46.648
We have been fighting period poverty,

00:01:46.648 --> 00:01:48.316
public health crises

00:01:48.316 --> 00:01:49.442
and the crisis of

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:51.653
missing and murdered Indigenous women.

00:01:51.653 --> 00:01:53.154
Most days,

00:01:53.154 --> 00:01:55.698
a lot of you don't hear too much about us.

00:01:56.199 --> 00:01:59.285
But as Women’s History Month comes to a close,

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.371
we’re here to remind you:

00:02:01.371 --> 00:02:04.833
It’s not just one month or one day.

00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:05.959
It’s every day.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.169
It’s not just the few.

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:09.963
It’s all of us.

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:13.258
We ignite a radiance uncontained by any spotlight.

00:02:14.259 --> 00:02:16.344
You're in its presence right now.

